## Explanation

This is a basic assignment on SQL injections. For the query, the `annotate` function adds another column to the select query. This column is the alias of the column that the user has provided.

As you see, the `annotate` function is vulnerable, because it accepts the user's input and appends it to the query. The query is vulnerable to SQL injection because it is vulnerable to:

* [SQL Injection](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SQL_injection)
* [Logic Injection](https://www.owasp.org/index.php/Logic_Injection)

The user can inject SQL code into the query after the alias by making use of semicolon characters. This can be done by passing the following query string as the `kwargs`:

```sql
SELECT * FROM table; DROP TABLE users;
```

For example, the `annotate` function will append the following to the query: `AS SELECT * FROM table; DROP TABLE users;`, which will be executed by the database.

In this case, we will drop the table that is intended to be kept in the database. If you want to test this code yourself, you can put the following string as the `kwargs`:

```sql
SELECT * FROM table; DELETE FROM users;
```

If you want your code to run normally, remove the semicolons from the alias.

