In Java, inheritance is implemented with the "extends" keyword.

When you are using this keyword, you have to remember to add "extends classname" in the beginning of your class declaration. The "classname" is the name of the class you are inheriting from.

To create a child class, you have to declare it with "public class ChildClass extends ParentClass".

In the child class, you can use any of the methods and attributes of the parent class.

When you want to use a method from the parent class that is not marked as "final", you have to mark your method "public".

This is because the child class can potentially override the method of the parent class, and it is a good practice to mark the method as "public" so that the child class will also be able to use this method.

